.TH WELLFETCH 1 "August 2026" "wellutils 1.2" "wellutils"
.SH NAME
wellfetch \- detailed system information display with an ANSI logo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wellfetch
[\fB\-\-lang\fR \fIru|en|auto\fR] [\fB\-\-color\fR \fIalways|auto|never\fR]
[\fB\-\-plain\fR|\fB\-\-box\fR] [\fB\-\-no\-emoji\fR]
[\fB\-\-no\-logo\fR|\fB\-\-logo\fR] [\fB\-\-full\fR]
[\fB\-\-logo\-width\fR \fIn\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wellfetch
prints detailed facts about the running system next to a colour logo rendered
from
.I /usr/share/wellutils/logo.png
using half-block characters.
.PP
Short sections (user, OS, kernel, uptime, shell, desktop, window manager,
terminal, theme, icons, font, packages, memory, swap, disk, resolution, audio,
locale, date) are grouped two per line for compactness; wide sections with
several values (CPU, GPUs, disks, monitors, peripherals) span a header line
plus indented continuation lines. Pass
.B \-\-full
to fall back to one section per line.
.PP
Sections cover the user, OS and architecture, kernel, uptime and boot time,
shell and its version, desktop environment, window manager, terminal, GTK
theme/icons/font, CPU model with core/thread count, frequency and load, every
GPU (with VRAM where nvidia-smi is available), memory and swap usage, the root
disk and each block device, the connected monitors with their resolutions and
the primary resolution, audio cards, USB peripherals grouped by type, package
counts, locale and the current date.
.PP
On a terminal the logo and a cyan label column are shown side by side.
Transparent logo pixels are emitted with the terminal's default background
attribute, so the real terminal background shows through and the artwork keeps
its natural square proportions. When the output is piped (or colour is
disabled) the report degrades to plain text with no escape sequences.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print version and exit.
.TP
.BI \-\-lang " ru|en|auto"
Output language. The default follows the system locale.
.TP
.BI \-\-color " always|auto|never"
Colourize output. \fBauto\fR means "only when attached to a terminal".
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Plain text, no logo, no colour.
.TP
.B \-\-box
Force the side-by-side layout even when piped (use with
.B \-\-color\fR \fBalways\fR).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-emoji
Drop emoji icons from the label column.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Machine-readable JSON on stdout; errors still go to stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-logo
Report text only, without the logo.
.TP
.B \-\-full
One section per line instead of the compact two-per-line grouping.
.TP
.B \-\-logo
Show the logo (default on a terminal).
.TP
.BI \-\-logo\-width " n"
Logo width in cells. Without it the width is derived from the terminal size.
.SH CONFIGURATION
If
.I ~/.config/wellutils/wellfetch.conf
(or the file named by
.B WELLFETCH_CONFIG
) exists it is sourced after the defaults. It may redefine any
.B wfv_\fIkey\fR
value function, set a
.B WF_VALUE_\fIkey\fR
override variable, or define
.B wf_custom
which prints extra
.RI "label|value"
lines. Example:
.PP
.nf
wf_custom() {
    printf 'Battery|95%%\\n'
}
.fi
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B COLUMNS
Terminal width used for the side-by-side layout.
.TP
.B WF_BG
Comma-separated RGB colour used to blend the few semi-transparent logo pixels.
Transparent pixels themselves are always emitted with the terminal default
background, so the logo shows through the real background without a painted
block.
.TP
.B WF_KEY
Alpha threshold (0\-\-255, default 40): pixels below it are treated as fully
transparent, dropping faint glow noise from the logo.
.TP
.B WELLFETCH_CONFIG
Path to an alternate configuration file.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/wellutils/wellfetch.conf
Optional user configuration.
.TP
.I /usr/share/wellutils/logo.png
Logo image.
.TP
.I /usr/share/wellutils/wfetch_art.py
PNG-to-ANSI renderer used by the tool.
.SH AUTHOR
wellbou_
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wellutils (1),
.BR wellper (1),
.BR wellhw (1)
